  "sources": [
    {
      "title": "Tropical land-use intensification reshapes biodiversity in expected and unexpected ways",
      "link": "https://www.nature.com/articles/d41586-026-03602-1",
      "description": "<p>Nature, Published online: 18 November 2026; <a href=\"https://www.nature.com/articles/d41586-026-03602-1\">doi:10.1038/d41586-026-03602-1</a></p>Global agricultural demand has increased over the past few decades, driving land-use intensification in some of the world’s most biodiverse regions. An assessment of some 40,000 species, from soil microorganisms to vertebrates, in Sumatra, Indonesia, reveals that agricultural intensification threatens biodiversity through species loss and changes in community composition across different spatial scales.",
      "source_name": "Nature Latest Research",
      "published": "2026-11-18T00:00:00"
    },
    {
      "title": "Tropical land-use intensification restructures biodiversity",
      "link": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-026-11298-6",
      "description": "<p>Nature, Published online: 18 November 2026; <a href=\"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-026-11298-6\">doi:10.1038/s41586-026-11298-6</a></p>Tropical land-use intensification drives substantial biodiversity loss and reorganizes community composition, with aboveground assemblages showing an increase in nestedness in monocultures compared with forests, underscoring the need for landscape-scale, multi-taxon conservation that integrates agricultural and natural habitats.",
      "source_name": "Nature Latest Research",
      "published": "2026-11-18T00:00:00"
    }
  ]
}